1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:06,762
Chapter 56: Renal and Urinary Tract Disorders.

3
00:00:06,762 --> 00:00:27,112
Pregnancy-Induced Urinary Tract Changes Urinary Tract Infections Nephrolithiasis Pregnancy After Renal Transplantation Polycystic Kidney Disease Glomerular Diseases Chronic Kidney Disease Acute Kidney Injury Lower Urinary Tract Lesions References Disorders of the kidneys and urinary tract are commonly encountered in pregnancy.

4
00:00:27,112 --> 00:00:31,650
Some precede pregnancy—one example is nephrolithiasis.

5
00:00:31,650 --> 00:00:41,787
Pregnancy-induced changes may predispose to the development or worsening of urinary tract disorders—an example is the markedly increased risk for pyelonephritis.

6
00:00:41,787 --> 00:00:47,800
Last, renal pathology unique to pregnancy, such as preeclampsia, can develop.

7
00:00:47,800 --> 00:00:56,787
In one Australian study, kidney disorders had an incidence of 0.3 percent in 407,580 births .

8
00:00:56,787 --> 00:01:00,312
Pregnancy-Induced Urinary Tract Changes.

9
00:01:00,312 --> 00:01:07,475
During normal pregnancy, significant changes in both structure and function take place in the urinary tract .

10
00:01:07,475 --> 00:01:13,400
The kidneys become larger, and dilation of the renal calyces and ureters can be striking.

11
00:01:13,400 --> 00:01:20,912
Some dilation develops before 14 weeks and likely is due to progesterone-induced relaxation of the muscularis.

12
00:01:20,912 --> 00:01:28,737
More marked dilation is apparent beginning in midpregnancy and stems from ureteral compression, especially on the right side.

13
00:01:28,737 --> 00:01:33,112
Vesicoureteral reflux also occurs during pregnancy.

14
00:01:33,112 --> 00:01:42,162
Important consequences of these physiological changes are an increased risk of upper urinary infection and erroneous interpretation of studies.

15
00:01:42,162 --> 00:01:47,875
Evidence of functional renal hypertrophy becomes apparent very soon after conception.

16
00:01:47,875 --> 00:01:52,212
Glomeruli are larger, although cell numbers are not greater .

17
00:01:52,212 --> 00:02:06,537
Intrarenal vasodilation lowers resistance of both afferent and efferent arterioles, and leads to higher effective renal plasma flow and glomerular filtration (Helal, 2012; Hussein, 2014).

18
00:02:06,537 --> 00:02:14,837
By 12 weeks’ gestation, the glomerular filtration rate (GFR) is already 20 percent above nonpregnant values .

19
00:02:14,837 --> 00:02:21,350
Ultimately, renal plasma flow rises by 40 percent and GFR by 65 percent.

20
00:02:21,350 --> 00:02:28,575
Consequently, serum concentrations of creatinine and urea decline substantively across pregnancy.

21
00:02:28,575 --> 00:02:36,525
Thus, values within a nonpregnant normal range may be abnormal for pregnancy (Appendix, p. 1232).

22
00:02:36,525 --> 00:02:42,887
Other alterations include those related to acid–base homeostasis and osmoregulation.

23
00:02:42,887 --> 00:02:46,787
# Assessment of Renal Function During Pregnancy.

24
00:02:46,787 --> 00:02:54,100
Urinalysis results are essentially unchanged during normal pregnancy, except for occasional glucosuria.

25
00:02:54,100 --> 00:03:02,875
In nondiabetic gravidas, glucosuria is thought to stem from a pregnancy-related reduced rate of renal tubular glucose reabsorption .

26
00:03:02,875 --> 00:03:11,600
This normal physiological change may add confusion and concerns for a gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) diagnosis.

27
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:19,737
Indeed, 1+ or greater urine dipstick readings have been associated with subsequent GDM diagnosis later in pregnancy.

28
00:03:19,737 --> 00:03:30,587
Despite this association, urine dipstick testing alone shows poor sensitivity to diagnose GDM (Buhlin, 2004; Olagbuji, 2015).

29
00:03:30,587 --> 00:03:44,775
For women with glucosuria, no guidelines in the United States direct practice, but in the United Kingdom, early oral glucose testing is considered for those with an isolated 2+ or repetitive 1+ urine dipstick readings .

30
00:03:44,775 --> 00:03:47,687
This seems to be a reasonable approach.

31
00:03:47,687 --> 00:03:54,925
Protein excretion is slightly elevated, but it seldom reaches levels that are detected by usual screening methods.

32
00:03:54,925 --> 00:04:08,650
Higby and colleagues (1994) reported 24-hour protein excretion in normal pregnancy to be 115 mg, with a 95-percent confidence level of 260 mg/d .

33
00:04:08,650 --> 00:04:12,612
This value did not significantly differ by trimester.

34
00:04:12,612 --> 00:04:20,762
Albumin constitutes only a small part of total protein excretion, and amounts range from 5 to 30 mg/d.

35
00:04:20,762 --> 00:04:30,775
From their review, Airoldi and Weinstein (2007) concluded that proteinuria must exceed 300 mg/d to be considered abnormal.

36
00:04:30,775 --> 00:04:36,750
Many consider 500 mg/d to be important with gestational hypertension.

37
00:04:36,750 --> 00:04:49,362
As an initial surrogate for 24-hour collections, quantification of the urinary protein-to-creatinine ratio in a spot urine sample is helpful in estimating a 24-hour protein excretion rate.

38
00:04:49,362 --> 00:04:55,712
The ratio is nearly the same numerically as the number of grams of protein excreted in urine per day.

39
00:04:55,712 --> 00:04:57,887
This measurement is

40
00:04:58,036 --> 00:05:01,073
best obtained from a first morning void.

41
00:05:01,023 --> 00:05:06,961
Surprisingly, women with lower urinary tract infections do not have more proteinuria .

42
00:05:06,961 --> 00:05:09,911
Blood is another urinalysis marker.

43
00:05:09,911 --> 00:05:22,511
In one study, 3 percent of 4307 nulliparas who were screened before 20 weeks’ gestation had idiopathic hematuria, defined as 1+ or greater blood on urine dipstick .

44
00:05:22,511 --> 00:05:27,111
These women had a twofold higher risk of developing preeclampsia.

45
00:05:27,111 --> 00:05:36,886
However, the link between preeclampsia and hematuria has not been found by all (Brown, 2005; Shahriaki, 2016).

46
00:05:36,886 --> 00:05:48,273
In another study of 1000 women screened during pregnancy, Brown and coworkers (2005) reported a 15percent incidence of dipstick microscopic hematuria.

47
00:05:48,273 --> 00:05:55,048
Most women had trace levels of hematuria, and the false-positive rate with this tool was 40 percent.

48
00:05:55,048 --> 00:06:03,948
Notably, other elements such as vitamin C, povidone-iodine, myoglobin, free hemoglobin can produce false-positive results.

49
00:06:03,948 --> 00:06:08,786
Thus, positive dipstick results should prompt formal urinalysis.

50
00:06:08,786 --> 00:06:16,473
Microscopic hematuria is defined as >3 red blood cells (RBCs) per high-powered field (HPF).

51
00:06:16,473 --> 00:06:23,486
For verified asymptomatic microscopic hematuria, few guidelines direct the best evaluation.

52
00:06:23,486 --> 00:06:27,236
History and physical often point to benign causes.

53
00:06:27,236 --> 00:06:33,036
If not, initial exclusion of infection by urinalysis or culture is reasonable.

54
00:06:33,036 --> 00:06:47,811
For early glomerular disease, serum creatinine and blood urea nitrogen levels, a spot protein-to-creatinine ratio or 24-hour urine collection, and urinalysis with examination of urine sediment can aid diagnosis.

55
00:06:47,811 --> 00:06:54,811
With this last step, proteinuria, dysmorphic RBCs, and cellular casts are characteristics.

56
00:06:54,811 --> 00:07:05,886
To help exclude neoplastic or stone disease, magnetic resonance urography (MRU) of the upper and lower urinary tract and cystoscopy is suitable in pregnancy .

57
00:07:05,886 --> 00:07:23,636
However, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2017) recommends against the last steps to exclude cancer for asymptomatic, low-risk, never-smoking women younger than 50 years who have ≤25 RBCs seen per HPF.

58
00:07:23,636 --> 00:07:35,048
If the serum creatinine level in pregnancy persistently exceeds 0.9 mg/dL (75 µmol/L), intrinsic renal disease should be suspected .

59
00:07:35,048 --> 00:07:40,798
In these cases, some determine the creatinine clearance as an estimate of the GFR.

60
00:07:40,798 --> 00:07:48,411
Of imaging tools, sonography informs on renal size, relative consistency, and elements of obstruction.

61
00:07:48,411 --> 00:07:52,923
Full-sequence intravenous pyelography is not done routinely.

62
00:07:52,923 --> 00:08:03,973
However, the clinical situation may warrant one-shot pyelography, in which contrast medium injection is followed after 30 minutes by one or two abdominal radiographs .

63
00:08:03,973 --> 00:08:13,111
Magnetic resonance (MR) imaging of renal masses provides excellent results due to its excellent resolution at soft-tissue interfaces .

64
00:08:13,111 --> 00:08:18,748
Cystoscopy is performed for the same indications as in the nonpregnant population.

65
00:08:18,748 --> 00:08:27,423
Ureteroscopy similarly may be indicated and carries an approximate 5percent complication rate during stone removal during pregnancy .

66
00:08:27,423 --> 00:08:34,936
Although relatively safe during pregnancy, renal biopsy usually is postponed unless results may change therapy.

67
00:08:34,936 --> 00:08:44,886
From a review of 243 biopsies in pregnant women, the incidence of complications was 7 percent compared with 1 percent in postpartum women .

68
00:08:44,886 --> 00:08:56,536
Lindheimer and colleagues (2013) recommend its consideration for those with rapid deterioration of renal function of unclear etiology or with symptomatic nephrotic syndrome.

69
00:08:56,536 --> 00:09:01,486
We have found biopsy helpful in selected cases to direct management.

70
00:09:01,486 --> 00:09:10,598
Renal biopsy performed in normal pregnant volunteers showed that approximately 50 percent had slight to moderate glomerular endotheliosis .

71
00:09:10,598 --> 00:09:17,848
In contrast, all 27 women with proteinuric hypertension had moderate to severe endotheliosis.

72
00:09:17,848 --> 00:09:21,623
# Pregnancy After Unilateral Nephrectomy.

73
00:09:21,623 --> 00:09:28,461
After removal of one kidney, a remaining normal kidney provides augmented compensatory renal function.

74
00:09:28,461 --> 00:09:31,848
With pregnancy, this is further amplified.

75
00:09:31,848 --> 00:09:39,161
Functional evaluation of the remaining kidney is essential, even though most women have no difficulty in pregnancy.

76
00:09:39,161 --> 00:09:46,473
At minimum, the serum creatinine level is measured and a urine spot protein:creatinine ratio is determined.

77
00:09:46,473 --> 00:09:52,361
No long-term permanent consequences accompany kidney donation done before pregnancy.

78
00:09:52,361 --> 00:09:54,311
However, the

79
00:09:54,460 --> 00:10:04,247
incidence of subsequent preeclampsia is greater (Davis, 2019; Steele, 2019; Vannevel, 2018).

80
00:10:04,197 --> 00:10:06,872
Urinary Tract Infections.

81
00:10:06,872 --> 00:10:13,235
Asymptomatic or covert bacteriuria is frequent in pregnancy and is an infection precursor.

82
00:10:13,235 --> 00:10:19,522
True infections of the urinary tract are the most prevalent bacterial infections during pregnancy.

83
00:10:19,522 --> 00:10:28,572
Symptomatic infection includes cystitis, or it may involve the renal calyces, pelvis, and parenchyma to cause pyelonephritis.

84
00:10:28,572 --> 00:10:33,397
Organisms from the normal perineal flora cause urinary infections.

85
00:10:33,397 --> 00:10:38,910
Escherichia coli strains most commonly cause nonobstructive pyelonephritis.

86
00:10:38,910 --> 00:10:45,585
In approximately 90 percent of these organisms, adhesins such as P- and S-fimbriae are present.

87
00:10:45,585 --> 00:10:57,385
These are cell-surface protein structures that enhance bacterial adherence to vaginal and uroepithelial cells and virulence (Hooton, 2012; Spurbeck, 2011).

88
00:10:57,385 --> 00:11:05,172
Maternal deaths have been attributed to E coli bearing Dr+ and P adhesins (Sledzińska, 2011).

89
00:11:05,172 --> 00:11:13,822
Urinary stasis, vesicoureteral reflux, and diabetes mellitus predispose to symptomatic upper urinary infections.

90
00:11:13,822 --> 00:11:19,985
Moreover, data suggest that pregnant women have more severe sequelae from urosepsis.

91
00:11:19,985 --> 00:11:27,660
As one influence, the T helper cell (Th1:Th2) ratio of normal pregnancy is reversed .

92
00:11:27,660 --> 00:11:33,010
Additionally, various perturbations of cytokine expression have been reported .

93
00:11:33,010 --> 00:11:39,447
In the puerperium, several risk factors also predispose a woman to urinary infections.

94
00:11:39,447 --> 00:11:47,910
Bladder sensitivity to intravesical fluid tension is often diminished as a consequence of labor trauma or conduction analgesia .

95
00:11:47,910 --> 00:11:57,572
Also, the sensation of bladder distention can be masked by discomfort from an episiotomy, periurethral laceration, or vaginal wall hematoma.

96
00:11:57,572 --> 00:12:09,022
Normal postpartum diuresis, while usually protective, may worsen bladder overdistention, and catheterization performed to relieve retention may lead to urinary infection.

97
00:12:09,022 --> 00:12:12,272
# Asymptomatic Bacteriuria.

98
00:12:12,272 --> 00:12:19,572
This refers to persistent, actively multiplying bacteria within the urinary tract in asymptomatic women.

99
00:12:19,572 --> 00:12:30,922
It is defined as one or more bacterial species that has a count ≥105 colony-forming units/mL determined by urine culture of a voided specimen .

100
00:12:30,922 --> 00:12:39,322
Its prevalence in sexually active nonpregnant women is 2 to 6 percent (Hooten, 2000; Nicolle, 2003).

101
00:12:39,322 --> 00:12:48,172
The highest incidence is in African-American multiparas with sickle-cell trait, and the lowest incidence is in affluent white women of low parity.

102
00:12:48,172 --> 00:12:52,972
Asymptomatic infection is also more common in women with diabetes .

103
00:12:52,972 --> 00:13:00,560
In most women, bacteriuria is recurrent or persistent, and thus it frequently is discovered during prenatal care.

104
00:13:00,560 --> 00:13:07,285
The incidence during pregnancy is similar to that in nonpregnant women and varies from 2 to 7 percent .

105
00:13:07,285 --> 00:13:12,735
Bacteriuria is typically already present at the time of the first prenatal visit.

106
00:13:12,735 --> 00:13:21,510
An initial positive urine culture result should prompt treatment, after which, fewer than 1 percent of women develop a symptomatic UTI .

107
00:13:21,510 --> 00:13:32,447
It may be prudent to treat lower concentrations, because pyelonephritis develops in some women with colony counts of only 20,000 to 50,000 organisms/mL .

108
00:13:32,447 --> 00:13:43,772
Significance If asymptomatic bacteriuria (ASB) is not treated, approximately 25 percent of affected women will develop symptomatic infection during pregnancy.

109
00:13:43,772 --> 00:13:49,522
Eradication of bacteriuria with antimicrobial agents prevents most of these.

110
00:13:49,522 --> 00:14:05,910
The American Academy of Pediatrics and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2017), as well as the U.S. Preventive Services Task Force (2019), recommend screening for bacteriuria at the first prenatal visit .

111
00:14:05,910 --> 00:14:10,872
Standard urine cultures may not be cost effective when the prevalence is low.

112
00:14:10,872 --> 00:14:18,122
Instead, less expensive screening tests such as the leukocyte esterase and nitrite dipstick are cost-effective .

113
00:14:18,122 --> 00:14:25,160
At Parkland Hospital, culture screening is done because of a 5- to 8percent prevalence of bacteriuria.

114
00:14:25,160 --> 00:14:30,922
Susceptibility determination is not necessary because initial treatment is empirical .

115
00:14:30,922 --> 00:14:40,372
Last, a special agar-coated dipstick culture technique has excellent positive and negative predictive values (Rogozińska, 2016).

116
00:14:40,372 --> 00:14:47,172
Other than pyelonephritis prevention, it is unclear whether ASB treatment provides other benefits.

117
00:14:47,172 --> 00:14:49,097
In many studies,

118
00:14:49,252 --> 00:14:52,864
there was no distinction from symptomatic infection.

119
00:14:52,814 --> 00:15:11,339
For example, in a cohort of 25,746 mother-newborn pairs, Schieve and coworkers (1994) reported UTI to be associated with increased risks for low-birthweight neonates, preterm delivery, pregnancyassociated hypertension, and anemia.

120
00:15:11,339 --> 00:15:21,314
These findings vary from those of Gilstrap and colleagues (1981b) and Whalley (1967), who studied asymptomatic infection.

121
00:15:21,314 --> 00:15:29,752
A California database study reported 160,000 women with UTIs were treated in the emergency department or hospital .

122
00:15:29,752 --> 00:15:38,764
These presumed symptomatic infections were associated with a 1.4-fold higher preterm delivery rate in those <37 weeks.

123
00:15:38,764 --> 00:15:47,652
Treatment Bacteriuria responds to empirical treatment with any of several antimicrobial regimens listed in Table 56-1.

124
00:15:47,652 --> 00:15:56,152
E coli is the most common species isolated in pregnant women (Le Blanc, 1964; Nicolle, 2003).

125
00:15:56,152 --> 00:16:00,127
Selection can be based on in vitro susceptibilities.

126
00:16:00,127 --> 00:16:11,364
However, in our extensive experience, empirical oral treatment for 10 days with nitrofurantoin macrocrystals, 100 mg at bedtime, is usually effective.

127
00:16:11,364 --> 00:16:22,302
Lumbiganon and associates (2009) reported satisfactory results with a 7-day oral course of nitrofurantoin, 100 mg given twice daily.

128
00:16:22,302 --> 00:16:28,252
Single-dose antimicrobial therapy also has been used with success for bacteriuria.

129
00:16:28,252 --> 00:16:35,289
The important caveat is that, regardless of regimen given, the recurrence rate approximates 30 percent.

130
00:16:35,289 --> 00:16:40,464
This may indicate covert upper tract infection and the need for longer therapy.

131
00:16:40,464 --> 00:16:43,064
Table 56-1.

132
00:16:43,064 --> 00:16:53,339
Oral Antimicrobial Agents Used for Treatment of Pregnant Women with Asymptomatic Bacteriuria Periodic surveillance is necessary to prevent recurrent UTIs .

133
00:16:53,339 --> 00:16:59,939
The first recurrence is considered a treatment failure and extended therapy for 21 days is considered.

134
00:16:59,939 --> 00:17:06,989
For women with persistent or frequent ASB, suppressive therapy for the remainder of pregnancy can be given.

135
00:17:06,989 --> 00:17:12,652
We routinely use nitrofurantoin, 100 mg orally at bedtime.

136
00:17:12,652 --> 00:17:17,764
Its rare but serious side effects are pulmonary and hepatic toxicity.

137
00:17:17,764 --> 00:17:20,939
# Cystitis and Urethritis.

138
00:17:20,939 --> 00:17:30,814
According to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, 7.2 percent of pregnant women were treated as an outpatient for a UTI in 2014 .

139
00:17:30,814 --> 00:17:38,714
Cystitis is characterized by dysuria, urgency, and frequency but causes few associated systemic findings.

140
00:17:38,714 --> 00:17:42,452
Pyuria and bacteriuria are usually found.

141
00:17:42,452 --> 00:17:49,614
Microscopic hematuria is common, and occasionally gross hematuria stems from hemorrhagic cystitis.

142
00:17:49,614 --> 00:17:57,139
Although cystitis is usually uncomplicated, the upper urinary tract may become involved by ascending infection.

143
00:17:57,139 --> 00:18:04,314
Almost 40 percent of pregnant women with acute pyelonephritis have preceding symptoms of lower tract infection .

144
00:18:04,314 --> 00:18:08,877
Women with cystitis respond readily to any of several regimens.

145
00:18:08,877 --> 00:18:15,389
Most of the three-day regimens listed in Table 56-1 are usually 90-percent effective.

146
00:18:15,389 --> 00:18:23,139
Single-dose therapy is less effective, and if it is used, concomitant pyelonephritis must be confidently excluded.

147
00:18:23,139 --> 00:18:32,002
Lower urinary tract symptoms with pyuria accompanied by a sterile urine culture may be from urethritis caused by Chlamydia trachomatis.

148
00:18:32,002 --> 00:18:38,202
Mucopurulent cervicitis usually coexists, and azithromycin therapy is effective .

149
00:18:38,202 --> 00:18:41,164
# Acute Pyelonephritis.

150
00:18:41,164 --> 00:18:48,177
Infection of the renal parenchyma is the most common serious nonobstetrical medical complication of pregnancy.

151
00:18:48,177 --> 00:18:58,239
One study of the Nationwide Inpatient Sample found that nearly 29,000 hospitalizations in 2006 were for acute pyelonephritis in pregnancy .

152
00:18:58,239 --> 00:19:06,989
The highest rates were noted for adolescents at 17.5 per 1000 and for Hispanic women at 10.1 per 1000.

153
00:19:06,989 --> 00:19:17,402
In another study of more than 70,000 pregnancies in a managed care organization, 3.5 percent of antepartum admissions were for urinary infections .

154
00:19:17,402 --> 00:19:28,227
The seriousness is underscored by the observations of Snyder and coworkers (2013) that pyelonephritis was the leading cause of septic shock during pregnancy.

155
00:19:28,227 --> 00:19:39,752
Moreover, in a 2-year audit of admissions to the Parkland Hospital Obstetrical Intermediate Care Unit, 12 percent of antepartum admissions were for sepsis caused by pyelonephritis .

156
00:19:39,752 --> 00:19:47,627
Of concern, maternal urosepsis may be related to an increased incidence of cerebral palsy in preterm newborns .

157
00:19:47,627 --> 00:19:50,302
Fortunately, data do not

158
00:19:50,452 --> 00:19:53,989
suggest serious long-term maternal sequelae .

159
00:19:53,939 --> 00:20:02,802
Clinical Findings Renal infection develops more frequently in the second half of pregnancy, and nulliparity and young age are risk factors .

160
00:20:02,802 --> 00:20:09,839
Pyelonephritis is unilateral and right-sided in more than half of cases, and it is bilateral in a fourth.

161
00:20:09,839 --> 00:20:16,964
Its abrupt onset is marked by fever, shaking chills, and aching pain in one or both lumbar regions.

162
00:20:16,964 --> 00:20:21,739
Anorexia, nausea, and vomiting may worsen dehydration.

163
00:20:21,739 --> 00:20:27,589
Tenderness usually can be elicited by percussion in one or both costovertebral angles.

164
00:20:27,589 --> 00:20:34,177
The urinary sediment contains many leukocytes, frequently in clumps, and numerous bacteria.

165
00:20:34,177 --> 00:20:44,052
The differential diagnosis includes, among others, labor, chorioamnionitis, appendicitis, placental abruption, or infarcted leiomyoma.

166
00:20:44,052 --> 00:20:48,864
Bacteremia is demonstrated in 15 to 20 percent of these women.

167
00:20:48,864 --> 00:21:11,802
E coli is isolated from urine or blood in 70 to 80 percent of infections, Klebsiella pneumoniae in 3 to 5 percent, Enterobacter or Proteus species in 3 to 5 percent, and gram-positive organisms, including group B Streptococcus and Staphylococcus aureus, in up to 10 percent of cases (Hill, 2005; Wing, 2000).

168
00:21:11,802 --> 00:21:18,564
Evidence of sepsis is common, and this is discussed in detail in Chapter 50 (p. 888).

169
00:21:18,564 --> 00:21:28,152
During care, plasma creatinine is monitored, because 5 percent of pregnant women develop renal dysfunction despite aggressive fluid resuscitation .

170
00:21:28,152 --> 00:21:34,552
Follow-up studies have demonstrated that this endotoxin-induced damage is reversible in the long term .

171
00:21:34,552 --> 00:21:51,777
In up to 10 percent of women, varying degrees of respiratory insufficiency, including frank pulmonary edema, may result from endotoxin-induced alveolar injury (Cunningham, 1987; Sheffield, 2005; Snyder, 2013).

172
00:21:51,777 --> 00:21:59,814
In some cases, pulmonary injury may be so severe that it causes acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS).

173
00:21:59,814 --> 00:22:05,214
Uterine activity from endotoxins is common and is related to fever severity.

174
00:22:05,214 --> 00:22:19,952
In a study by Millar and associates (2003), women with pyelonephritis averaged 5 contractions per hour at admission, and this declined to 2 per hour within 6 hours of intravenous fluid and antimicrobial administration.

175
00:22:19,952 --> 00:22:30,864
β-agonist therapy for tocolysis raises the likelihood of respiratory insufficiency from permeability edema because of the sodiumand fluid-retaining properties of those agents .

176
00:22:30,864 --> 00:22:41,539
In one study, the incidence of pulmonary edema in women with pyelonephritis who were given β-agonists was 8 percent—a fourfold increase compared with that expected .

177
00:22:41,539 --> 00:22:49,102
Endotoxin-induced hemolysis is common, and approximately a third of patients with pyelonephritis develop anemia .

178
00:22:49,102 --> 00:22:56,877
With recovery, hemoglobin regeneration is normal because acute infection does not affect erythropoietin production .

179
00:22:56,877 --> 00:23:03,377
Management One scheme for management of acute pyelonephritis is shown in Table 56-2.

180
00:23:03,377 --> 00:23:13,064
Although we obtain blood cultures when the patient temperature exceeds 39°C, prospective trials show them to be of limited clinical utility .

181
00:23:13,064 --> 00:23:19,027
Intravenous hydration to ensure adequate urinary output is the cornerstone of treatment.

182
00:23:19,027 --> 00:23:26,964
Antimicrobials also are begun promptly with the caution that they may initially worsen endotoxemia from bacterial lysis.

183
00:23:26,964 --> 00:23:37,927
Ongoing surveillance for worsening of sepsis is monitored by serial determinations of urinary output, blood pressure, pulse, temperature, and oxygen saturation.

184
00:23:37,927 --> 00:23:42,289
High fever should be lowered with a cooling blanket and acetaminophen.

185
00:23:42,289 --> 00:23:48,977
This is especially important in early pregnancy because of possible teratogenic effects of hyperthermia.

186
00:23:48,977 --> 00:23:51,577
Table 56-2.

187
00:23:51,577 --> 00:24:13,652
Management of the Pregnant Woman with Acute Pyelonephritis Antimicrobial therapy usually is empirical, and ampicillin plus gentamicin; cefazolin or ceftriaxone; or an extended-spectrum antibiotic were all 95-percent effective in randomized trials (Sanchez-Ramos, 1995; Wing, 1998, 2000).

188
00:24:13,652 --> 00:24:25,552
Fewer than half of E coli strains are sensitive to ampicillin in vitro, but gentamicin plus ampicillin or plus a cephalosporin generally have a synergistic effect and excellent activity .

189
00:24:25,552 --> 00:24:30,402
Serum creatinine levels are monitored if nephrotoxic drugs are given.

190
00:24:30,402 --> 00:24:35,289
Initial treatment at Parkland Hospital is ampicillin plus gentamicin.

191
00:24:35,289 --> 00:24:37,577
Some recommend suitable

192
00:24:37,732 --> 00:24:42,157
substitutes if bacterial studies show in vitro resistance.

193
00:24:42,107 --> 00:24:53,619
With any of the regimens discussed, response is usually prompt, and 95 percent of women are afebrile by 72 hours (Hill, 2005; Wing, 2000).

194
00:24:53,619 --> 00:25:02,969
After discharge, most recommend oral therapy to complete 10 to 14 days (Hooton, 2012; Johnson, 2018).

195
00:25:02,969 --> 00:25:12,594
Recurrent infection—either covert or symptomatic—is common and develops in 30 to 40 percent of women following completion of pyelonephritis treatment.

196
00:25:12,594 --> 00:25:24,769
Unless other measures are taken to ensure urine sterility, nitrofurantoin, 100 mg orally at bedtime given for the remainder of the pregnancy, reduces bacteriuria recurrence.

197
00:25:24,769 --> 00:25:35,344
Persistent Infection Generally, stepwise defervescence of approximately 1°F per day follows intravenous hydration and antimicrobial therapy.

198
00:25:35,344 --> 00:25:40,057
Upwards of 20 percent of women are febrile for more than 4 days .

199
00:25:40,057 --> 00:25:52,457
With persistent spiking fever or lack of clinical improvement by 48 to 72 hours, urinary tract obstruction, calculi, or abscess is considered ( Johnson, 2018).

200
00:25:52,457 --> 00:25:57,107
In these women, renal sonography is recommended to search for these.

201
00:25:57,107 --> 00:26:02,544
Obstruction manifests by abnormal ureteral or pyelocaliceal dilation.

202
00:26:02,544 --> 00:26:11,332
Nephrolithiasis, however, is not always seen in a renal sonogram (Butler, 2000; Maikranz, 1987).

203
00:26:11,332 --> 00:26:21,044
If stones are strongly suspected despite a nondiagnostic sonographic examination, a plain abdominal radiograph will identify nearly 90 percent.

204
00:26:21,044 --> 00:26:27,469
Another option is the modified one-shot intravenous pyelography (p. 995) .

205
00:26:27,469 --> 00:26:37,544
Last, persistent infection can be due to an intrarenal or perinephric abscess or phlegmon (Cox, 1988; Rafi, 2012).

206
00:26:37,544 --> 00:26:43,469
MR imaging is preferred for abscess detection if renal sonography is nonconclusive .

207
00:26:43,469 --> 00:26:51,619
Obstruction relief is important, and we have found that percutaneous nephrostomy is preferable because the stents are more easily replaced.

208
00:26:51,619 --> 00:26:56,482
Another method is cystoscopic placement of a double-J ureteral stent.

209
00:26:56,482 --> 00:27:03,257
These stents are usually left in place until after delivery and often become encrusted and need replacing .

210
00:27:03,257 --> 00:27:09,457
Last, in some women, surgical removal of stones may be needed (p. 1000).

211
00:27:09,457 --> 00:27:22,444
Renal abscesses larger than 3 cm may necessitate percutaneous drainage or surgical treatment (Coelho, 2007; Mandal, 2017; Siegel, 1996).

212
00:27:22,444 --> 00:27:33,732
Outpatient Management of Pyelonephritis This is an option for nonpregnant women with uncomplicated pyelonephritis (Fox, 2017; Johnson, 2018).

213
00:27:33,732 --> 00:27:46,419
Wing and colleagues (1999) described such management in 92 pregnant women who were first given in-hospital intramuscular ceftriaxone, two 1-g doses 24 hours apart.

214
00:27:46,419 --> 00:27:59,769
At this point, one third of the group was considered suitable for outpatient therapy, and these women were randomly assigned either to discharge and oral antimicrobials or to continued hospitalization with intravenous therapy.

215
00:27:59,769 --> 00:28:05,932
A third of the outpatient management group was unable to adhere to the treatment regimen and was admitted.

216
00:28:05,932 --> 00:28:13,369
These findings suggest that outpatient management of pyelonephritis is applicable only to very few pregnant women.

217
00:28:13,369 --> 00:28:16,182
# Reflux Nephropathy.

218
00:28:16,182 --> 00:28:26,144
This refers to loss of nephron mass from patchy interstitial scarring and tubular atrophy due to high-pressure reflux of sterile urine from the bladder to the kidneys.

219
00:28:26,144 --> 00:28:33,569
In adults, long-term complications include hypertension, which may be severe if renal damage is demonstrable .

220
00:28:33,569 --> 00:28:49,869
Moreover, reports describing 879 pregnancies in 432 women with reflux nephropathy indicate that impaired renal function and bilateral renal scarring were associated with higher rates of pregnancy-associated hypertensive disorders .

221
00:28:49,869 --> 00:28:57,482
Many also had surgical correction of reflux as children, and these women commonly have bacteriuria when pregnant .

222
00:28:57,482 --> 00:29:08,682
In other women with reflux nephropathy, no clear history implicates recurrent cystitis, acute pyelonephritis, or obstructive disease (Gebäck, 2016).

223
00:29:08,682 --> 00:29:15,182
Chronic renal disease and pregnancy outcome is discussed further later (p. 1003).

224
00:29:15,182 --> 00:29:17,257
Nephrolithiasis.

225
00:29:17,257 --> 00:29:24,444
Kidney stones develop in 9 percent of women during their lifetime with an average age of onset in the third decade .

226
00:29:24,444 --> 00:29:31,669
Calcium salts make up approximately 80 percent of stones, and hyperparathyroidism should be excluded.

227
00:29:31,669 --> 00:29:35,219
Calcium oxalate stones are most common in

228
00:29:35,380 --> 00:29:44,667
young nonpregnant women, but in pregnancy most stones—65 to 75 percent—are calcium phosphate or hydroxyapatite .

229
00:29:44,617 --> 00:29:50,180
Nephrolithiasis is more common in women with prior pregnancies than in nulligravidas.

230
00:29:50,180 --> 00:30:00,342
Normal physiological changes of the urinary system may promote stone formation, and elevation of urinary pH reduces calcium phosphate solubility .

231
00:30:00,342 --> 00:30:05,905
More than half of patients who have a stone typically form another stone within 10 years.

232
00:30:05,905 --> 00:30:11,180
Contrary to past teachings, a low-calcium diet promotes stone formation.

233
00:30:11,180 --> 00:30:18,067
Current recommendations to prevent recurrences include hydration and a diet low in sodium and protein .

234
00:30:18,067 --> 00:30:22,117
Thiazide diuretics also diminish stone formation.

235
00:30:22,117 --> 00:30:29,542
In general, obstruction, infection, intractable pain, and heavy bleeding are indications for stone removal.

236
00:30:29,542 --> 00:30:39,142
Extraction by a flexible basket via cystoscopy, although used less often than in the past, is still a reasonable consideration for pregnant women.

237
00:30:39,142 --> 00:30:46,192
In nonpregnant patients, stone destruction by lithotripsy is preferred to surgical therapy in most cases.

238
00:30:46,192 --> 00:30:52,905
Limited information guides the use of lithotripsy during pregnancy, and it is not generally recommended.

239
00:30:52,905 --> 00:30:56,080
# Stone Disease During Pregnancy.

240
00:30:56,080 --> 00:31:01,242
The reported incidence of stone disease complicating pregnancy varies widely.

241
00:31:01,242 --> 00:31:13,867
At the low end, Butler and coworkers (2000) found an incidence of 0.3 admissions per 1000 pregnancies in more than 186,000 deliveries at Parkland Hospital.

242
00:31:13,867 --> 00:31:21,530
In an Israeli study, the incidence in nearly 220,000 pregnancies was 0.8 per 1000 .

243
00:31:21,530 --> 00:31:27,105
Most stone episodes are diagnosed in the second and third trimesters of pregnancy .

244
00:31:27,105 --> 00:31:37,292
Bladder stones are rare, but recurrent infection and labor obstructed by stones have been reported (Ait Benkaddour, 2006; Ruan, 2011).

245
00:31:37,292 --> 00:31:43,930
Data are conflicting whether women with kidney stones have an increased risk for adverse pregnancy outcomes.

246
00:31:43,930 --> 00:31:55,330
Swartz and colleagues (2007) reported a preterm delivery rate of 10.6 percent in women with nephrolithiasis compared with 6.4 percent in normal controls.

247
00:31:55,330 --> 00:32:04,205
A nationwide case-control study from Taiwan also reported 20- to 40-percent increases in low-birthweight newborns and preterm births .

248
00:32:04,205 --> 00:32:14,705
To the contrary, a case-control study from Hungary reported that pregnancy outcomes, including preterm delivery, were similar in women with and without stone disease .

249
00:32:14,705 --> 00:32:19,305
Comparable conclusions were drawn from the Israeli study noted earlier .

250
00:32:19,305 --> 00:32:26,630
Women who have formed stones prior to pregnancy have a higher incidence of gestational diabetes and preeclampsia .

251
00:32:26,630 --> 00:32:32,517
Diagnosis More than 90 percent of pregnant women with nephrolithiasis present with pain.

252
00:32:32,517 --> 00:32:41,080
However, some evidence suggests that pregnant women may have fewer symptoms with stone passage because of normal urinary tract dilation .

253
00:32:41,080 --> 00:32:51,367
Gross hematuria is less common than in nonpregnant women and has an incidence of 2 to 23 percent (Butler, 2000; Lewis, 2003).

254
00:32:51,367 --> 00:32:58,455
Sonography is the first-line study to visualize stones, but as discussed above, many are undetected .

255
00:32:58,455 --> 00:33:06,830
Abnormal urinary tract dilation or absent ureteral “jets” of urine into the bladder are indirect indicators of urolithiasis .

256
00:33:06,830 --> 00:33:13,567
If abnormal dilation is seen but no stone is visualized, one-shot pyelography may be useful.

257
00:33:13,567 --> 00:33:16,180
Figure 56-1.

258
00:33:16,180 --> 00:33:24,105
A. Sonographic image of the right upper quadrant demonstrates severe hydronephrosis and thinned echogenic renal parenchyma.

259
00:33:24,105 --> 00:33:32,730
B. Application of color Doppler during sonography of the bladder reveals a urine jet (red streak) from the left ureteral orifice.

260
00:33:32,730 --> 00:33:36,855
No flow was noted from the right orifice during observation.

261
00:33:36,855 --> 00:33:45,605
C. Sagittal CT image shows an obstructing stone in the mid right ureter (arrow) and hydroureteronephrosis (arrow head ).

262
00:33:45,605 --> 00:33:52,380
Helical computed tomography (CT) scanning is preferred imaging for nonpregnant individuals.

263
00:33:52,380 --> 00:33:59,717
In pregnancy, MR imaging is the second-line test following a nondiagnostic sonographic evaluation .

264
00:33:59,717 --> 00:34:11,480
However, if CT imaging is needed, White and colleagues (2007) recommend unenhanced helical CT and cite an average fetal radiation dose to be 7 mGy.

265
00:34:11,480 --> 00:34:15,792
Management Treatment depends on symptoms and gestational age .

266
00:34:15,792 --> 00:34:19,530
Intravenous hydration and analgesics are given.

267
00:34:19,530 --> 00:34:23,942
Patients strain collected urine to identify passed stones.

268
00:34:24,100 --> 00:34:30,850
In half of women with symptomatic stones, infection will be identified, and this is treated vigorously.

269
00:34:30,800 --> 00:34:41,012
Although calculi infrequently cause symptomatic obstruction during pregnancy, persistent pyelonephritis should prompt a search for obstruction due to nephrolithiasis.

270
00:34:41,012 --> 00:34:49,937
Approximately 75 percent of symptomatic women will improve with conservative therapy, and the stone usually passes spontaneously .

271
00:34:49,937 --> 00:35:04,712
Others require an invasive procedure such as ureteral stenting, ureteroscopy, percutaneous nephrostomy, transureteral laser lithotripsy, or basket extraction (Butler, 2000; Hosseini, 2017).

272
00:35:04,712 --> 00:35:14,025
Watterson and coworkers (2002) described successful transureteral holmium:YAG laser lithotripsy in nine of 10 women.

273
00:35:14,025 --> 00:35:22,612
Ultimately, less than 2 percent of symptomatic pregnant women require surgical exploration following one of the procedures listed above .

274
00:35:22,612 --> 00:35:27,875
The need for fluoroscopy limits the utility of percutaneous nephrolithotomy.

275
00:35:27,875 --> 00:35:33,037
Extracorporeal shock-wave lithotripsy is contraindicated in pregnancy.

276
00:35:33,037 --> 00:35:36,287
Pregnancy After Renal Transplantation.

277
00:35:36,287 --> 00:35:47,450
In 2020, more than 100,000 registrants were waiting for renal transplantations through the Organ Procurement and Transplantation Network —OPTN (2020).

278
00:35:47,450 --> 00:35:55,000
The 1-year graft survival rate is 95 percent for those from living donors and is 90 percent from deceased donors.

279
00:35:55,000 --> 00:36:07,862
Survival rates approximately doubled in the 1990s, due in large part to the introduction of cyclosporine and muromonab-CD3 (Orthoclone OKT3) to prevent and treat organ rejection.

280
00:36:07,862 --> 00:36:13,412
The latter is a monoclonal antibody against the CD3 receptor of T cells.

281
00:36:13,412 --> 00:36:20,087
Since then, mycophenolate mofetil and tacrolimus have further reduced acute rejection episodes.

282
00:36:20,087 --> 00:36:23,337
However, the former is teratogenic .

283
00:36:23,337 --> 00:36:32,100
In the report from the National Transplant Pregnancy Registry, 23 percent of fetuses exposed to mycophenolate had birth defects .

284
00:36:32,100 --> 00:36:39,912
Importantly, resumption of renal function after transplantation promptly restores fertility in reproductive-aged women .

285
00:36:39,912 --> 00:36:46,625
Thus, these women should be counseled to use contraception, especially those treated with mycophenolate.

286
00:36:46,625 --> 00:36:53,687
In one study, more than half of transplant recipients reported that they were not counseled regarding contraception .

287
00:36:53,687 --> 00:36:56,425
# Pregnancy Outcomes.

288
00:36:56,425 --> 00:37:01,612
Women with solid organ transplant have higher severe maternal morbidity rates .

289
00:37:01,612 --> 00:37:11,050
Deshpande and associates (2011) reviewed the outcomes of more than 4700 pregnancies in renal transplant recipients.

290
00:37:11,050 --> 00:37:15,887
Approximately 70 percent of pregnancies resulted in a live birth.

291
00:37:15,887 --> 00:37:23,162
However, rates of preeclampsia, gestational diabetes, and preterm delivery were substantially greater.

292
00:37:23,162 --> 00:37:28,687
Similar outcomes were described for the Australian and New Zealand Transplant Registry .

293
00:37:28,687 --> 00:37:38,875
In the United Kingdom, Bramham and colleagues (2013) identified a live-birth rate that exceeded 90 percent in renal transplant recipients.

294
00:37:38,875 --> 00:37:45,500
Half were delivered before 37 weeks’ gestation, but only 9 percent before 32 weeks.

295
00:37:45,500 --> 00:37:52,312
Low birthweight complicated half of pregnancies, and growth restriction complicated 25 percent.

296
00:37:52,312 --> 00:37:59,775
Notably, the incidence of fetal malformations was not increased, except in those who took mycophenolate mofetil.

297
00:37:59,775 --> 00:38:04,562
The incidence of preeclampsia is high in all transplant recipients.

298
00:38:04,562 --> 00:38:14,012
In the UK National Cohort Study reported by Bramham and associates (2013), the incidence of preeclampsia was 22 percent.

299
00:38:14,012 --> 00:38:23,512
From their review, Josephson and McKay (2011) cite an incidence of one third of pregnancies but question the validity of this frequency.

300
00:38:23,512 --> 00:38:29,475
Importantly, in some cases, rejection is difficult to distinguish from preeclampsia.

301
00:38:29,475 --> 00:38:34,900
That said, the incidence of rejection episodes approximates only 2 percent .

302
00:38:34,900 --> 00:38:42,225
Viral infections—especially polyomavirus hominis 1, also called BK virus—are frequent.

303
00:38:42,225 --> 00:38:47,500
In addition, gestational diabetes is found in approximately 5 percent.

304
00:38:47,500 --> 00:38:51,337
Both are likely related to immunosuppression therapy.

305
00:38:51,337 --> 00:39:02,162
Similar outcomes have been reported by several other investigators (Al Duraihimh, 2008; Cruz Lemini, 2007; Ghafari, 2008).

306
00:39:02,162 --> 00:39:11,350
The Italian Society of Nephrology recommends that women who have undergone transplantation satisfy several requisites before attempting pregnancy .

307
00:39:11,350 --> 00:39:14,937
First, women should be in good general health for

308
00:39:15,100 --> 00:39:18,800
at least 1 to 2 years after transplantation.

309
00:39:18,750 --> 00:39:21,975
Second, renal function should be stable.

310
00:39:21,975 --> 00:39:40,862
Parameters include a GFR ≥60 mL/min, serum creatinine level <2 mg/dL, and proteinuria <500 mg/d. Third, there should be no recent evidence for graft rejection and no treatment with teratogenic drugs.

311
00:39:40,862 --> 00:39:45,000
Last, hypertension should be absent or well controlled.

312
00:39:45,000 --> 00:39:51,012
Cyclosporine and tacrolimus are given routinely to renal transplantation recipients.

313
00:39:51,012 --> 00:39:59,562
Coincident with expected pregnancy-related intravascular volume expansion, cyclosporine blood levels decline during pregnancy.

314
00:39:59,562 --> 00:40:04,687
However, this was not reported to be associated with rejection episodes .

315
00:40:04,687 --> 00:40:10,650
Unfortunately, these agents are nephrotoxic and may also cause renal hypertension.

316
00:40:10,650 --> 00:40:20,887
In fact, they likely contribute substantively to the chronic renal disease that develops in 10 to 20 percent of patients with nonrenal solid organ transplantation .

317
00:40:20,887 --> 00:40:28,250
Concern persists regarding the possible late effects in offspring subjected to immunosuppressive therapy in utero.

318
00:40:28,250 --> 00:40:34,825
These include malignancy, germ cell dysfunction, and malformations in the children of the offspring.

319
00:40:34,825 --> 00:40:38,912
In addition, cyclosporine is secreted in breast milk .

320
00:40:38,912 --> 00:40:50,037
In two small studies, these children had normal neurological and intellectual function (MoralesBuenrostro, 2019; Schreiber-Zamora, 2019).

321
00:40:50,037 --> 00:40:56,862
Last, pregnancy-induced renal hyperfiltration theoretically may impair long-term graft survival.

322
00:40:56,862 --> 00:41:07,512
However, Sturgiss and Davison (1995) found no evidence for this in one study of 34 allograft recipients followed for a mean of 15 years.

323
00:41:07,512 --> 00:41:09,737
# Management.

324
00:41:09,737 --> 00:41:12,412
Close surveillance is necessary.

325
00:41:12,412 --> 00:41:20,000
Covert bacteriuria is treated, and if it is recurrent, suppressive treatment is given for the remainder of the pregnancy.

326
00:41:20,000 --> 00:41:28,137
Serial hepatic enzyme concentrations and blood counts are monitored for toxic effects of azathioprine and cyclosporine.

327
00:41:28,137 --> 00:41:32,200
Some recommend measurement of serum cyclosporine levels.

328
00:41:32,200 --> 00:41:44,425
Gestational diabetes is more common if corticosteroids are taken, and in these cases overt diabetes must be excluded with glucose tolerance testing done at the initiation of prenatal care.

329
00:41:44,425 --> 00:41:54,050
Surveillance for opportunistic infections from herpesvirus, cytomegalovirus, and toxoplasmosis is important because these infections are common.

330
00:41:54,050 --> 00:42:02,300
Some recommend surveillance for BK virus in women known to be infected, however, diagnosis and treatment are problematic.

331
00:42:02,300 --> 00:42:08,600
Renal function is monitored, and the GFR usually increases 20 to 25 percent.

332
00:42:08,600 --> 00:42:14,750
If a significant rise in the serum creatinine level is detected, its cause must be determined.

333
00:42:14,750 --> 00:42:23,475
Possibilities include acute rejection, cyclosporine toxicity, preeclampsia, infection, and urinary tract obstruction.

334
00:42:23,475 --> 00:42:29,587
Evidence of pyelonephritis or graft rejection should prompt admission for aggressive management.

335
00:42:29,587 --> 00:42:33,737
Imaging studies and kidney biopsy may be indicated.

336
00:42:33,737 --> 00:42:42,062
The woman is carefully monitored for development or worsening of underlying hypertension, and especially of superimposed preeclampsia.

337
00:42:42,062 --> 00:42:48,437
Management of hypertension during pregnancy is the same as for patients without an organ transplant .

338
00:42:48,437 --> 00:42:55,887
Vigilant fetal surveillance is indicated because of the higher incidences of fetal-growth restriction and preterm delivery.

339
00:42:55,887 --> 00:43:13,712
Although cesarean delivery is reserved for obstetrical indications, occasionally the transplanted kidney obstructs labor, and the overall cesarean delivery rate approaches 80 percent (Bramham, 2013; Madej, 2018; Rocha, 2013).

340
00:43:13,712 --> 00:43:16,262
Polycystic Kidney Disease.

341
00:43:16,262 --> 00:43:22,062
This usually autosomally dominant systemic disease primarily affects the kidneys.

342
00:43:22,062 --> 00:43:30,725
Its basic pathophysiology is a ciliopathy that affects renal tubules, although only approximately 5 percent develop cysts .

343
00:43:30,725 --> 00:43:39,537
The incidence is about 1 in 1000 births and it causes approximately 5 to 10 percent of end-stage renal disease in the United States.

344
00:43:39,537 --> 00:43:52,962
Although genetically heterogeneous, almost 85 percent of cases are due to PKD1 gene mutations on chromosome 16, and the other 15 percent to PKD2 mutations on chromosome 4.

345
00:43:52,962 --> 00:44:00,987
Prenatal diagnosis is available if the mutation has been identified in a family member or if linkage has been established in the family.

346
00:44:00,987 --> 00:44:05,462
Preimplantation genetic screening reduces the risk of affected

347
00:44:05,620 --> 00:44:08,307
fetuses to 1 to 2 percent .

348
00:44:08,257 --> 00:44:15,707
Renal complications more commonly affect men than women, and symptoms usually appear in the third or fourth decade.

349
00:44:15,707 --> 00:44:24,270
Flank pain, hematuria, proteinuria, abdominal masses, and associated calculi and infection are frequent findings.

350
00:44:24,270 --> 00:44:30,807
Hypertension develops in 75 percent, and progression to renal failure is a major problem.

351
00:44:30,807 --> 00:44:37,845
Superimposed acute renal failure also may develop from infection or obstruction from ureteral angulation.

352
00:44:37,845 --> 00:44:44,132
Specifically, renal cyst may displace and create angles along the normal ureteral course.

353
00:44:44,132 --> 00:44:46,995
Other organs are commonly involved.

354
00:44:46,995 --> 00:44:52,782
Asymptomatic hepatic cysts coexist in a third of patients with polycystic kidneys.

355
00:44:52,782 --> 00:44:57,470
Hepatic involvement is more common and more aggressive in women than in men.

356
00:44:57,470 --> 00:45:05,807
Approximately 10 percent of patients with polycystic kidney disease die from rupture of an associated intracranial berry aneurysm.

357
00:45:05,807 --> 00:45:15,757
Up to a fourth of patients have cardiac valvular lesions, which include mitral valve prolapse and mitral, aortic, and tricuspid valvular incompetence.

358
00:45:15,757 --> 00:45:18,495
# Pregnancy Outcomes.

359
00:45:18,495 --> 00:45:27,070
The prognosis for pregnancy in women with polycystic kidney disease depends on the degree of associated hypertension and renal insufficiency.

360
00:45:27,070 --> 00:45:29,832
Urinary infections are common.

361
00:45:29,832 --> 00:45:44,420
Chapman and coworkers (1994) compared pregnancy outcomes in 235 affected women who had 605 pregnancies with those of 108 unaffected family members who had 244 pregnancies.

362
00:45:44,420 --> 00:45:56,395
Composite perinatal complication rates were similar—33 versus 26 percent —but hypertension, including preeclampsia, was more common in women with polycystic kidneys.

363
00:45:56,395 --> 00:46:00,732
Pregnancy does not seem to accelerate the natural disease course .

364
00:46:00,732 --> 00:46:02,882
Glomerular Diseases.

365
00:46:02,882 --> 00:46:10,582
The glomerulus and its capillaries are subject to numerous conditions and agents that can lead to acute and chronic diseases.

366
00:46:10,582 --> 00:46:18,507
Glomerular damage can be caused by toxins or infections or from systemic disorders such as hypertension or diabetes.

367
00:46:18,507 --> 00:46:21,245
It may also be idiopathic.

368
00:46:21,245 --> 00:46:30,607
When there is capillary inflammation, the process is termed glomerulonephritis, and in many of these cases, an autoimmune process is involved.

369
00:46:30,607 --> 00:46:38,757
Glomerular disease or glomerulonephritis may result from a single stimulus such as that following group A streptococcal infections.

370
00:46:38,757 --> 00:46:46,095
It may also be a manifestation of a multisystem disease such as systemic lupus erythematosus or diabetes .

371
00:46:46,095 --> 00:46:51,345
Persistent glomerulonephritis eventually leads to decline of renal function.

372
00:46:51,345 --> 00:46:58,057
Progression is variable and often does not become apparent until chronic renal insufficiency is diagnosed.

373
00:46:58,057 --> 00:47:05,707
Lewis and Neilsen (2018) group glomerular injuries into six syndromes based on clinical patterns .

374
00:47:05,707 --> 00:47:16,057
Some underlying disorders—examples include infections, vasculitides, and diabetes—can result in one clinical pattern in different individuals.

375
00:47:16,057 --> 00:47:26,582
Last, disorders within each of these categories may be seen in young women, and thus, these may already be established before pregnancy or may first manifest then.

376
00:47:26,582 --> 00:47:29,145
Table 56-3.

377
00:47:29,145 --> 00:47:47,420
Patterns of Clinical Glomerulonephritis As with any renal disorder, goals to achieve optimal pregnancy outcomes aim to stabilize rapid disease progression, minimize proteinuria with use of appropriate immunosuppressive agents, control hypertension, and delay pregnancy until these are achieved.

378
00:47:47,420 --> 00:47:50,357
# Acute Nephritic Syndromes.

379
00:47:50,357 --> 00:47:54,995
Acute glomerulonephritis may result from any of several causes .

380
00:47:54,995 --> 00:48:03,157
The clinical presentation usually includes hypertension, hematuria, red-cell casts, pyuria, and proteinuria.

381
00:48:03,157 --> 00:48:11,645
Varying degrees of renal insufficiency and salt and water retention result in edema, hypertension, and circulatory congestion .

382
00:48:11,645 --> 00:48:16,607
The prognosis and treatment of nephritic syndromes depends on their etiology.

383
00:48:16,607 --> 00:48:20,232
Some recede spontaneously or with treatment.

384
00:48:20,232 --> 00:48:27,395
However, in some patients, rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis leads to end-stage renal failure.

385
00:48:27,395 --> 00:48:34,257
In others, chronic glomerulonephritis develops and manifests as slowly progressive renal disease.

386
00:48:34,257 --> 00:48:44,420
The prototype is acute poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis, which is historically interesting because it was confused with eclampsia until the mid1800s.

387
00:48:44,420 --> 00:48:50,907
Immunoglobulin A (IgA) nephropathy, also known as Berger disease, is the most common

388
00:48:51,076 --> 00:48:54,713
form of acute glomerulonephritis worldwide .

389
00:48:54,663 --> 00:49:03,326
The isolated form of IgA nephropathy occurs sporadically, and it may be related to Henoch-Schönlein purpura as the systemic form.

390
00:49:03,326 --> 00:49:11,613
Another isolated nephritis type may be due to anti–glomerular basement membrane antibodies, which are typically IgG.

391
00:49:11,613 --> 00:49:21,451
These IgG antibodies may also involve the lungs to manifest as a pulmonary-renal syndrome with alveolar hemorrhage, which is termed Goodpasture syndrome.

392
00:49:21,451 --> 00:49:28,576
Pregnancy Acute nephritic syndromes during pregnancy can be difficult to differentiate from severe preeclampsia.

393
00:49:28,576 --> 00:49:34,776
One example is systemic lupus erythematosus with a flare during the second half of pregnancy .

394
00:49:34,776 --> 00:49:45,713
In some cases, renal biopsy may be necessary to determine etiology and direct management (Lindheimer, 2013; Ramin, 2006).

395
00:49:45,713 --> 00:49:50,551
This is discussed further in Chapter 62 (p. 1112).

396
00:49:50,551 --> 00:49:57,363
Whatever the underlying etiology, glomerular diseases have profound effects on pregnancy outcome.

397
00:49:57,363 --> 00:50:10,076
In an older study, Packham and coworkers (1989) described 395 pregnancies in 238 women with primary glomerulonephritis diagnosed before pregnancy.

398
00:50:10,076 --> 00:50:20,701
The most common lesions identified by biopsy were membranous glomerulonephritis, IgA glomerulonephritis, and diffuse mesangial glomerulonephritis.

399
00:50:20,701 --> 00:50:33,151
Although most of these women had normal renal function, half developed hypertension, a fourth was delivered preterm, and the perinatal mortality rate after 28 weeks’ gestation was 80 per 1000.

400
00:50:33,151 --> 00:50:42,763
As expected, the worst perinatal outcomes were in women with impaired renal function, early or severe hypertension, and nephrotic-range proteinuria.

401
00:50:42,763 --> 00:50:48,051
O’Shaughnessy and colleagues (2017) confirmed these findings.

402
00:50:48,051 --> 00:51:03,738
In 48 pregnancies complicated by glomerulonephritis, a third developed preeclampsia, 39 percent had a doubling of protein excretion, and 27 percent had a ≥50-percent rise in serum creatinine levels.

403
00:51:03,738 --> 00:51:09,526
Half of the pregnancies ended in preterm birth and 13 percent in perinatal death.

404
00:51:09,526 --> 00:51:15,126
Similar outcomes have been reported for pregnancies in women with IgA nephropathy.

405
00:51:15,126 --> 00:51:29,513
From one review of 376 pregnancies, investigators reported a perinatal mortality rate of 12-percent; preterm delivery, 9 percent; low birthweight, 10 percent; and preeclampsia, 7 percent .

406
00:51:29,513 --> 00:51:34,226
Progression of renal insufficiency during pregnancy is controversial.

407
00:51:34,226 --> 00:51:47,326
From their reports, Su and coworkers (2017), but not Wang and colleagues (2019), found that women with IgA nephropathy had renal insufficiency progression during pregnancy.

408
00:51:47,326 --> 00:51:50,051
# Nephrotic Syndromes.

409
00:51:50,051 --> 00:51:54,163
Heavy proteinuria is the hallmark of the nephrotic syndromes.

410
00:51:54,163 --> 00:52:05,926
Several primary and secondary kidney disorders cause immunological- or toxicmediated injury and glomerular capillary-wall breakdown that allows excessive filtration of plasma proteins.

411
00:52:05,926 --> 00:52:14,976
In addition to heavy urine protein excretion, the syndrome is characterized by hypoalbuminemia, hypercholesterolemia, and edema.

412
00:52:14,976 --> 00:52:18,001
Hypertension is frequently comorbid.

413
00:52:18,001 --> 00:52:24,513
This, coupled with albumin nephrotoxicity, contributes to eventual renal insufficiency.

414
00:52:24,513 --> 00:52:30,638
Some of the more common causes of the nephrotic syndrome are listed in Table 56-4.

415
00:52:30,638 --> 00:52:38,588
Treatment depends on etiology, and renal biopsy will disclose microscopic abnormalities that may help direct care.

416
00:52:38,588 --> 00:52:42,713
Edema is problematic, especially during pregnancy.

417
00:52:42,713 --> 00:52:52,763
Normal amounts of dietary protein of high biological value are encouraged, and this practice contrasts with high-protein diets, which increase proteinuria.

418
00:52:52,763 --> 00:53:00,963
The incidence of thromboembolism is greater and varies with the severity of hypertension, proteinuria, and renal insufficiency .

419
00:53:00,963 --> 00:53:08,263
Renal vein thrombosis is particularly worrisome, although both arterial and venous thromboses may develop.

420
00:53:08,263 --> 00:53:18,663
Explanations for this risk are platelet hyperactivity, urinary loss of antithrombotic factors, and increased production of prothrombotic factors by the liver .

421
00:53:18,663 --> 00:53:30,851
Some recommend that women with a serum albumin <2 g/dL or 24-hour urinary protein excretion >3.5 g should be given thromboprophylaxis throughout pregnancy .

422
00:53:30,851 --> 00:53:40,451
Some cases of nephrosis from primary glomerular disease respond to glucocorticosteroids and other immunosuppressants or cytotoxic drug therapy.

423
00:53:40,451 --> 00:53:43,413
In most cases caused by infection

424
00:53:43,564 --> 00:53:48,739
or drugs, proteinuria recedes once the underlying cause is corrected.

425
00:53:48,689 --> 00:53:51,276
Table 56-4.

426
00:53:51,276 --> 00:54:01,139
Causes of the Nephrotic Syndrome in Adults Pregnancy Maternal and perinatal outcomes in women with a nephrotic syndrome depend on its underlying cause and severity.

427
00:54:01,139 --> 00:54:09,276
Whenever possible, these should be ascertained, and renal biopsy may be indicated to determine if there is a treatable etiology.

428
00:54:09,276 --> 00:54:16,176
Half of women with nephrotic-range proteinuria will have higher daily protein excretion as pregnancy progresses .

429
00:54:16,176 --> 00:54:24,751
In women with nephrosis cared for at Parkland Hospital, we reported that two thirds had protein excretion that exceeded 3 g/d .

430
00:54:24,751 --> 00:54:34,314
At the same time, however, if these women had only mild degrees of renal dysfunction, they displayed a normally augmented GFR across pregnancy .

431
00:54:34,314 --> 00:54:44,239
Management of edema during pregnancy can be particularly challenging, as it is intensified by normally rising hydrostatic pressure in the lower extremities.

432
00:54:44,239 --> 00:54:53,526
In some women, massive vulvar edema may develop in those with comorbid conditions such as diabetes, syphilis, preeclampsia, and others .

433
00:54:53,526 --> 00:55:00,601
Another major problem is that up to half of these women with nephrosis have chronic hypertension that may require treatment .

434
00:55:00,601 --> 00:55:08,776
In these, as well as in previously normotensive women, preeclampsia is common and often develops early in pregnancy .

435
00:55:08,776 --> 00:55:11,364
Figure 56-2.

436
00:55:11,364 --> 00:55:17,076
Massive vulvar edema in a pregnant woman with marked proteinuria due to preeclampsia.

437
00:55:17,076 --> 00:55:25,451
Most women with a nephrotic syndrome who do not have severe hypertension or renal insufficiency will have successful pregnancy outcomes.

438
00:55:25,451 --> 00:55:33,401
Conversely, if there is renal insufficiency, moderate to severe hypertension, or both, the prognosis is much worse.

439
00:55:33,401 --> 00:55:41,226
Our experiences caring for such women with 65 pregnancies at Parkland Hospital showed that complications are frequent .

440
00:55:41,226 --> 00:55:49,014
Protein excretion during pregnancy averaged 4 g/d, and a third of the women had classic nephrotic syndrome.

441
00:55:49,014 --> 00:55:58,851
Some degree of renal insufficiency was seen in 75 percent, chronic hypertension in 40 percent, and persistent anemia in 25 percent.

442
00:55:58,851 --> 00:56:05,851
Importantly, preeclampsia developed in 60 percent, and 45 percent had preterm deliveries.

443
00:56:05,851 --> 00:56:12,126
Even so, after excluding abortions, 53 of 57 neonates were born alive.

444
00:56:12,126 --> 00:56:22,714
De Castro and associates (2017) reported similar results from 26 pregnancies complicated by a mean protein excretion of 8 g/d.

445
00:56:22,714 --> 00:56:31,864
Long-Term Outcomes Women identified to have nephrotic syndromes either before or during pregnancy are at risk for serious long-term adverse outcomes.

446
00:56:31,864 --> 00:56:40,264
Of the women cared for at Parkland Hospital, at least 20 percent of women followed for 10 years progressed to end-stage renal failure .

447
00:56:40,264 --> 00:56:48,276
Similarly, Chen and associates (2001) reported short-term outcomes in 15 women with nephrotic syndromes.

448
00:56:48,276 --> 00:56:57,051
By 2 years, three of these women had died, three had developed chronic renal failure, and two had progressed to end-stage renal disease.

449
00:56:57,051 --> 00:57:09,251
Women with serum creatinine levels >1.4 mg/dL and 24-hour protein excretion >1 g/d have the shortest renal survival times following pregnancy .

450
00:57:09,251 --> 00:57:11,526
Chronic Kidney Disease.

451
00:57:11,526 --> 00:57:17,526
This describes a pathophysiological process that can progress to end-stage renal disease.

452
00:57:17,526 --> 00:57:27,326
The National Kidney Foundation (2019) describes six stages of chronic kidney disease (CKD) defined by decreasing GFR.

453
00:57:27,326 --> 00:57:43,226
It progresses from stage 0, in which GFR is ≥90 mL/min/1.73 m2, to stage 5, in which GFR is <15 mL/min/1.73 m2.

454
00:57:43,226 --> 00:57:50,876
Several diseases result in progressively declining renal function, including the glomerular diseases discussed earlier.

455
00:57:50,876 --> 00:58:03,989
Those that most commonly lead to end-stage disease requiring dialysis and kidney transplantation include diabetes mellitus, chronic hypertension, glomerulonephritis, and polycystic kidney disease .

456
00:58:03,989 --> 00:58:12,189
Most reproductive-aged women with these just-mentioned diseases have varying degrees of renal insufficiency, proteinuria, or both.

457
00:58:12,189 --> 00:58:20,789
To counsel regarding fertility and pregnancy outcome, the degree of renal functional impairment and of associated hypertension are assessed.

458
00:58:20,789 --> 00:58:28,889
In general, successful pregnancy outcome may be more related to these two factors than to the specific underlying renal disorder.

459
00:58:28,889 --> 00:58:32,564
An overall prognosis can be estimated by

460
00:58:32,716 --> 00:58:37,878
considering women with CKD in arbitrary categories of renal function .

461
00:58:37,828 --> 00:58:59,716
These include normal or mild impairment— defined as a serum creatinine <1.5 mg/dL; moderate impairment—defined as a serum creatinine 1.5 to 3.0 mg/dL; and severe renal insufficiency— defined as a serum creatinine >3.0 mg/dL.

462
00:58:59,716 --> 00:59:13,816
Some recommend using these older categories, although others suggest adopting the classification of the National Kidney Foundation described above (Davison, 2011; Piccoli, 2010, 2011).

463
00:59:13,816 --> 00:59:17,691
Thus, obstetricians must be familiar with both.

464
00:59:17,691 --> 00:59:21,191
# Pregnancy and Chronic Kidney Disease.

465
00:59:21,191 --> 00:59:29,266
The severity of renal insufficiency, along with any underlying hypertension, strongly influences pregnancy outcome.

466
00:59:29,266 --> 00:59:40,041
Additionally, comorbidities secondary to a systemic disorder—for example, diabetes or systemic lupus erythematosus—portend a worse prognosis .

467
00:59:40,041 --> 00:59:49,678
For all women with CKD, the incidences of hypertension and preeclampsia, preterm and growth-restricted neonates, and other problems are high.

468
00:59:49,678 --> 00:59:56,366
Low-dose aspirin starting at 12 to 28 weeks is recommended to help lower preeclampsia rates .

469
00:59:56,366 --> 01:00:04,441
Loss of renal tissue is associated with compensatory intrarenal vasodilation and hypertrophy of the surviving nephrons.

470
01:00:04,441 --> 01:00:13,366
The resultant hyperperfusion and hyperfiltration eventually damage surviving nephrons to cause nephrosclerosis and worsening renal function.

471
01:00:13,366 --> 01:00:20,753
With mild renal insufficiency, pregnancy causes greater augmentation of renal plasma flow and GFR .

472
01:00:20,753 --> 01:00:26,891
With progressively worsening renal function, renal plasma flow is augmented little, if any.

473
01:00:26,891 --> 01:00:37,791
In one study, only half of women with moderate renal insufficiency demonstrated pregnancy-augmented glomerular filtration, and women with severe disease had no increase .

474
01:00:37,791 --> 01:00:44,491
Importantly, chronic renal insufficiency also curtails normal pregnancy-induced hypervolemia.

475
01:00:44,491 --> 01:00:52,891
Blood volume expansion during pregnancy is related to disease severity and correlates inversely with serum creatinine concentration.

476
01:00:52,891 --> 01:01:03,716
As shown in Figure 56-3, women with mild to moderate renal dysfunction have blood volume expansion that averages 55 percent above nonpregnant volumes.

477
01:01:03,716 --> 01:01:14,291
However, with severe renal insufficiency, volume expansion averages only 25 percent, which is similar to that seen with hemoconcentration from eclampsia.

478
01:01:14,291 --> 01:01:20,666
In addition, these women have variable degrees of chronic anemia due to intrinsic renal disease.

479
01:01:20,666 --> 01:01:27,778
Thus, they are at risk for hypovolemia and acute blood loss anemia with even normal blood loss at delivery.

480
01:01:27,778 --> 01:01:30,366
Figure 56-3.

481
01:01:30,366 --> 01:01:53,091
Blood volume expansion in 44 normally pregnant women at term compared with 29 who had eclampsia; 10 with moderate chronic renal insufficiency (CRI)—serum creatinine 1.5 to 2.9 mg/dL; and four with severe CRI—serum creatinine ≥3.0 mg/dL.

482
01:01:53,091 --> 01:01:57,978
Data from Cunningham, 1990; Zeeman, 2009.

483
01:01:57,978 --> 01:02:09,053
Renal Disease with Preserved Function In some women, although glomerular disease has not yet caused renal insufficiency, incidences of pregnancy complications are still increased.

484
01:02:09,053 --> 01:02:18,141
As shown in Table 56-5, these problems are less frequent than in cohorts of women with moderate and severe renal insufficiency.

485
01:02:18,141 --> 01:02:21,003
Two older studies illustrate this.

486
01:02:21,003 --> 01:02:31,203
In one, Surian and colleagues (1984) described outcomes of 123 pregnancies in women with biopsy-proven glomerular disease.

487
01:02:31,203 --> 01:02:38,766
Although only a few of these women had renal dysfunction, 40 percent developed obstetrical or renal complications.

488
01:02:38,766 --> 01:02:54,666
In another study of 395 pregnancies in women with preexisting glomerulonephritis and minimal renal insufficiency, impaired renal function developed in 15 percent of these women during pregnancy, and 60 percent had worsening proteinuria .

489
01:02:54,666 --> 01:03:02,978
Only 12 percent had antecedent chronic hypertension, however, more than half of the pregnancies were complicated by hypertension.

490
01:03:02,978 --> 01:03:14,591
The perinatal mortality rate was 140 per 1000, and even without early-onset or severe hypertension or nephrotic-range proteinuria, the rate was 50 per 1000.

491
01:03:14,591 --> 01:03:20,278
Importantly, in 5 percent of these women, worsening renal function was permanent.

492
01:03:20,278 --> 01:03:22,966
Table 56-5.

493
01:03:22,966 --> 01:03:25,478
Complications (%)

494
01:03:25,478 --> 01:03:33,816
Associated with Chronic Kidney Disease During Pregnancy Chronic Renal Insufficiency As noted, pregnancy complication rates are higher

495
01:03:33,964 --> 01:03:40,251
in women with CKD and renal insufficiency compared with women who have preserved renal function.

496
01:03:40,201 --> 01:03:46,226
Furthermore, adverse outcomes are generally directly related to the degree of renal impairment.

497
01:03:46,226 --> 01:03:56,151
Of the more recent reports shown in Table 56-5, outcomes of women with moderate or severe renal insufficiency are usually not separated.

498
01:03:56,151 --> 01:04:14,626
A systematic review of 1514 pregnancies in women with CKD described increased odds ratios of preeclampsia, 10.4; preterm delivery, 6.3; fetal-growth restriction, 4.9; and cesarean delivery, 2.7 .

499
01:04:14,626 --> 01:04:27,564
Other investigators have described pregnancies complicated by moderate or severe renal insufficiency (Cunningham, 1990; Imbasciati, 2007; Jones, 1996).

500
01:04:27,564 --> 01:04:38,626
Despite a high incidence of chronic hypertension, anemia, preeclampsia, preterm delivery, and fetal-growth restriction, perinatal outcomes were generally acceptable.

501
01:04:38,626 --> 01:04:46,314
As shown in Figure 56-4, fetal growth is frequently impaired and related to renal dysfunction severity.

502
01:04:46,314 --> 01:04:48,989
Figure 56-4.

503
01:04:48,989 --> 01:05:10,189
Birthweight percentiles of infants born to 29 women at Parkland Hospital with mild to moderate renal insufficiency—serum creatinine 1.4–2.4 mg/dL (black points) and severe renal insufficiency—serum creatinine ≥2.5 mg/dL (red points).

504
01:05:10,189 --> 01:05:15,289
Data from Cunningham, 1990; Stettler, 1992.

505
01:05:15,289 --> 01:05:20,201
Growth curves are those reported by Alexander, 1996.

506
01:05:20,201 --> 01:05:22,426
# Management.

507
01:05:22,426 --> 01:05:27,826
Prenatal care for women with CKD incorporates several important aspects.

508
01:05:27,826 --> 01:05:37,114
Frequent monitoring of blood pressure is paramount, and serum creatinine levels and 24-hour protein excretion are quantified as indicated.

509
01:05:37,114 --> 01:05:43,064
Bacteriuria is treated to lower the risk of pyelonephritis and further nephron loss.

510
01:05:43,064 --> 01:05:46,476
Protein-restricted diets are not recommended.

511
01:05:46,476 --> 01:05:53,851
In some women with anemia from chronic renal insufficiency, a response is seen with recombinant erythropoietin.

512
01:05:53,851 --> 01:05:57,614
However, hypertension is a common side effect.

513
01:05:57,614 --> 01:06:01,614
Serial sonography is performed to follow fetal growth.

514
01:06:01,614 --> 01:06:08,176
The differentiation between worsening hypertension and superimposed preeclampsia is problematic.

515
01:06:08,176 --> 01:06:25,001
Preliminary data indicate that the angiogenic biomarkers placental growth factor (PlGF) and its soluble receptor, soluble fms-like tyrosine kinase 1 (sFlt-1), may be useful to separate chronic from gestational hypertension .

516
01:06:25,001 --> 01:06:29,401
This is described in Chapter 40 (p. 694).

517
01:06:29,401 --> 01:06:32,026
# Long-Term Effects.

518
01:06:32,026 --> 01:06:40,201
Pregnancy may accelerate CKD progression by augmenting hyperfiltration and glomerular pressure to worsen nephrosclerosis .

519
01:06:40,201 --> 01:06:44,801
Women with severe renal insufficiency have greater susceptibility.

520
01:06:44,801 --> 01:06:57,339
Jungers and associates (1995) reported few long-term pregnancy-related adverse effects in 360 women with chronic glomerulonephritis and antecedent normal renal function.

521
01:06:57,339 --> 01:07:07,826
In a study from Parkland Hospital, 20 percent of pregnant women with moderate to severe renal insufficiency developed end-stage renal failure by a mean of 4 years .

522
01:07:07,826 --> 01:07:15,239
He and coworkers (2018) confirmed these findings in women who had stage 3 or 4 CKD.

523
01:07:15,239 --> 01:07:20,539
As noted, progression is common for many women with chronic renal disorders.

524
01:07:20,539 --> 01:07:31,614
At 1 year after pregnancy, Jones and Hayslett (1996) reported that 10 percent of the women had developed end-stage renal failure—stage 5 CKD.

525
01:07:31,614 --> 01:07:39,564
Similar findings in women with a median follow-up of 3 years were described by Imbasciati and colleagues (2007).

526
01:07:39,564 --> 01:07:52,664
By this time, end-stage disease was apparent in 30 percent of women whose prepregnancy serum creatinine was ≥1.4 mg/dL and who had proteinuria >1 g/d.

527
01:07:52,664 --> 01:07:58,076
Chronic proteinuria is also a marker for subsequent development of renal failure.

528
01:07:58,076 --> 01:08:07,189
In one study, 20 percent of women with chronic proteinuria discovered during pregnancy progressed to end-stage renal failure within several years .

529
01:08:07,189 --> 01:08:15,039
Another study reported that an equal number of nonpregnant women had identical loss of function when similar durations were compared .

530
01:08:15,039 --> 01:08:20,751
It may be that pregnancy does not hasten an already predetermined declining renal function.

531
01:08:20,751 --> 01:08:23,901
# Dialysis During Pregnancy.

532
01:08:23,901 --> 01:08:34,839
Significantly impaired renal function is accompanied by subfertility that may be corrected with chronic renal replacement therapy—either hemodialysis or peritoneal dialysis .

533
01:08:34,839 --> 01:08:40,176
Not unexpectedly, maternal complications are common and include severe

534
01:08:40,348 --> 01:08:45,123
hypertension, placental abruption, heart failure, and sepsis.

535
01:08:45,073 --> 01:08:54,048
To illustrate this, the outcomes of 233 pregnancies in women undergoing dialysis are shown in Table 56-6.

536
01:08:54,048 --> 01:09:03,498
The high incidences of hypertension, low birthweight, and preterm delivery result in a perinatal mortality rate of 250 per 1000.

537
01:09:03,498 --> 01:09:14,860
Piccoli and associates (2016) performed a systematic review and described 681 pregnancies in 647 women undergoing dialysis.

538
01:09:14,860 --> 01:09:21,573
Of these, 616 had hemodialysis and 65 had peritoneal dialysis.

539
01:09:21,573 --> 01:09:31,898
They chronicled a high incidence of hypertension, preterm delivery, and fetal-growth restriction, and the perinatal mortality was 180 per 1000.

540
01:09:31,898 --> 01:09:37,923
They found an inverse relationship between hours of dialysis per week and adverse outcomes.

541
01:09:37,923 --> 01:09:43,685
Last, they reported that hemodialysis was superior to peritoneal dialysis.

542
01:09:43,685 --> 01:09:46,348
Table 56-6.

543
01:09:46,348 --> 01:09:59,160
Pregnancy Outcomes in Women Undergoing Dialysis During Pregnancy For the woman already undergoing either method of dialysis, it seems reasonable to continue that method with consideration for its increasing frequency.

544
01:09:59,160 --> 01:10:05,535
In the woman who has never been dialyzed, the threshold for initiation during pregnancy is unclear.

545
01:10:05,535 --> 01:10:15,185
Hladunewich and colleagues (2016) recommend consideration for dialysis when the creatinine clearance is 20 mL/min or less.

546
01:10:15,185 --> 01:10:23,973
Extension of dialysis frequency to five to six times weekly may be necessary to avoid abrupt volume changes that cause hypotension.

547
01:10:23,973 --> 01:10:33,223
Hladunewich and coworkers (2011) recommend attention to certain protocols that include replacement of substances lost through dialysis.

548
01:10:33,223 --> 01:10:41,723
Multivitamin doses are doubled, and oral calcium and iron salts are provided along with sufficient dietary protein and calories.

549
01:10:41,723 --> 01:10:45,373
Chronic anemia is treated with erythropoietin.

550
01:10:45,373 --> 01:10:51,898
To meet pregnancy changes, extra calcium is added to the dialysate along with less bicarbonate.

551
01:10:51,898 --> 01:10:54,248
Acute Kidney Injury.

552
01:10:54,248 --> 01:11:09,198
Previously termed acute renal failure, acute kidney injury (AKI) is now used to describe impairment of kidney function over days to weeks that leads to retention of nitrogenous and other waste products normally excreted by the kidneys.

553
01:11:09,198 --> 01:11:29,173
Current definitions include a rise in a serum creatinine level of at least 0.3 mg/dL within 48 hours, or ≥50 percent increase from baseline within a week, or urine output reduced to <0.5 mL/kg per hour for >6 hours .

554
01:11:29,173 --> 01:11:33,948
Criteria for diagnosis in pregnancy have not been standardized .

555
01:11:33,948 --> 01:11:43,410
Most studies use a definition in which serum creatinine levels rise ≥0.3 mg/dL from baseline.

556
01:11:43,410 --> 01:11:48,910
Animal studies have shown that pregnancy protects the kidney from acute ischemic injury .

557
01:11:48,910 --> 01:11:58,260
Although the incidence of AKI in pregnancy has dropped substantially, it still occasionally causes significant obstetrical morbidity and mortality.

558
01:11:58,260 --> 01:12:07,148
In a Canadian study, the incidence was reported to be 2.68 per 10,000 births between 2009 and 2010 .

559
01:12:07,148 --> 01:12:21,598
Outcomes are available from four older studies comprising a total of 266 women with AKI (Drakeley, 2002; Nzerue, 1998; Sibai, 1990; Turney, 1989).

560
01:12:21,598 --> 01:12:30,323
Approximately 70 percent had preeclampsia, 50 percent had obstetrical hemorrhage, and 30 percent had a placental abruption.

561
01:12:30,323 --> 01:12:37,223
Almost 20 percent required dialysis, and the maternal mortality rate approximated 15 percent.

562
01:12:37,223 --> 01:12:55,473
Although obstetrical cases of AKI requiring dialysis are less frequent today, acute renal ischemia is still commonly associated with severe preeclampsia and hemorrhage (Conti-Ramsden, 2019; Jim, 2017; Prakash, 2018).

563
01:12:55,473 --> 01:13:04,498
Notable contributors are placental abruption and hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, and low platelet count (HELLP) syndrome.

564
01:13:04,498 --> 01:13:08,735
Sepsis is another, especially in resource-poor countries .

565
01:13:08,735 --> 01:13:13,748
AKI also often complicates acute fatty liver of pregnancy.

566
01:13:13,748 --> 01:13:23,660
Nelson and colleagues (2013) reported some degree of renal insufficiency in virtually all of 52 such women cared for at Parkland Hospital .

567
01:13:23,660 --> 01:13:29,760
Dehydration caused by severe hyperemesis gravidarum also can lead to AKI .

568
01:13:29,760 --> 01:13:33,885
Other causes include thrombotic microangiopathies .

569
01:13:33,885 --> 01:13:36,910
# Diagnosis and Management.

570
01:13:36,910 --> 01:13:44,973
In most women, renal failure develops postpartum, thus management is usually not complicated by fetal considerations.

571
01:13:44,973 --> 01:13:51,173
Diagnostically, an acute rise in serum creatinine levels is most often due to renal

572
01:13:51,340 --> 01:13:53,015
ischemia.

573
01:13:52,965 --> 01:13:58,102
But oliguria also is an important sign of acutely impaired renal function.

574
01:13:58,102 --> 01:14:04,152
In obstetrical cases, both prerenal and intrarenal factors commonly coincide.

575
01:14:04,152 --> 01:14:15,690
For example, with total placental abruption, severe hypovolemia results from massive hemorrhage, and frequently associated preeclampsia causes preexistent renal ischemia .

576
01:14:15,690 --> 01:14:22,840
When azotemia is severe and oliguria persists, some form of renal replacement treatment may be indicated.

577
01:14:22,840 --> 01:14:31,477
Hemofiltration or dialysis is initiated before patients markedly deteriorate, and hemodynamic measurements are normalized.

578
01:14:31,477 --> 01:14:37,677
Medication dose adjustments are imperative, and magnesium sulfate is a prominent example .

579
01:14:37,677 --> 01:14:45,252
Early dialysis appears to reduce the mortality rate appreciably and may enhance the extent of renal function recovery.

580
01:14:45,252 --> 01:14:50,152
With time, renal function usually returns to normal or near normal.

581
01:14:50,152 --> 01:14:57,327
Even so, women in subsequent pregnancies have higher incidences of hypertension and adverse fetal outcomes .

582
01:14:57,327 --> 01:14:59,477
# Prevention.

583
01:14:59,477 --> 01:15:06,727
AKI in obstetrics is most often due to acute blood loss and especially that associated with preeclampsia.

584
01:15:06,727 --> 01:15:23,427
Thus, it may often be prevented by the following means: 1. Prompt and vigorous volume replacement with crystalloid solutions and blood in instances of massive hemorrhage, such as in placental abruption, placenta previa, uterine rupture, and postpartum uterine atony .

585
01:15:23,427 --> 01:15:33,902
2. Delivery or termination of pregnancies complicated by severe preeclampsia or eclampsia, and careful blood transfusion if blood loss is more than average .

586
01:15:33,902 --> 01:15:44,515
3. Close observation for early signs of sepsis and shock in women with pyelonephritis, septic abortion, chorioamnionitis, or other pelvic infections .

587
01:15:44,515 --> 01:15:53,590
4. Avoidance of loop diuretics to treat oliguria before ensuring that blood volume and cardiac output are adequate for renal perfusion.

588
01:15:53,590 --> 01:16:03,565
5. Judicious use of vasoconstrictor drugs to treat hypotension, and only after it has been determined that pathological vasodilation is the cause.

589
01:16:03,565 --> 01:16:11,477
Irreversible ischemic renal failure caused by acute cortical necrosis has become exceedingly uncommon in obstetrics.

590
01:16:11,477 --> 01:16:22,540
Before widespread availability of dialysis, it complicated a fourth of obstetrical renal failure cases (Grünfeld, 1987; Turney, 1989).

591
01:16:22,540 --> 01:16:29,490
Most cases followed placental abruption, preeclampsia-eclampsia, and endotoxin-induced shock.

592
01:16:29,490 --> 01:16:34,965
Once common with septic abortion, this is a rare cause in the United States today .

593
01:16:34,965 --> 01:16:41,652
Histologically, the lesion appears to result from thrombosis of segments of the renal vascular system.

594
01:16:41,652 --> 01:16:46,215
The lesions may be focal, patchy, confluent, or gross.

595
01:16:46,215 --> 01:16:57,015
Clinically, renal cortical necrosis follows the course of acute renal failure, and its differentiation from acute tubular necrosis is not possible during the early phase.

596
01:16:57,015 --> 01:17:00,865
The prognosis depends on the extent of the necrosis.

597
01:17:00,865 --> 01:17:06,327
Recovery of function is variable, and stable renal insufficiency may result.

598
01:17:06,327 --> 01:17:09,340
# Obstructive Renal Failure.

599
01:17:09,340 --> 01:17:18,415
Rarely, bilateral ureteral compression by a very large pregnant uterus obstructs ureters to cause severe oliguria and azotemia.

600
01:17:18,415 --> 01:17:22,290
An example is shown in Figure 56-5.

601
01:17:22,290 --> 01:17:30,452
Brandes and Fritsche (1991) reviewed 13 cases that were the consequence of a markedly overdistended uterus.

602
01:17:30,452 --> 01:17:40,302
They described a woman with twins who developed anuria and a serum creatinine level of 12.2 mg/dL at 34 weeks’ gestation.

603
01:17:40,302 --> 01:17:50,027
After amniotomy, urine flow resumed at 500 mL/h, and a rapid decline in serum creatinine levels to normal range followed.

604
01:17:50,027 --> 01:17:57,527
Eckford and Gingell (1991) described 10 women in whom ureteral obstruction was relieved by stenting.

605
01:17:57,527 --> 01:18:04,315
The stents were left in place for a mean of 15.5 weeks and removed 4 to 6 weeks postpartum.

606
01:18:04,315 --> 01:18:07,015
Figure 56-5.

607
01:18:07,015 --> 01:18:15,152
A. Magnetic resonance image in a coronal plane of a pregnant woman with unilateral hydronephrosis caused by ureteral obstruction.

608
01:18:15,152 --> 01:18:25,090
The serum creatinine was 8 mg/dL and decreased to 0.8 mg/dL after a percutaneous nephrostomy tube was placed.

609
01:18:25,090 --> 01:18:33,015
B. Left kidney (arrow) and associated hydronephrosis (asterisk) are again noted in this axial plane image.

610
01:18:33,015 --> 01:18:36,790
We have observed this phenomenon on several occasions.

611
01:18:36,790 --> 01:18:44,340
In our experience, women with previous urinary tract surgery for reflux are more likely to have such obstructions .

612
01:18:44,500 --> 01:18:50,812
Partial ureteral obstruction may be accompanied by fluid retention and significant hypertension.

613
01:18:50,762 --> 01:18:56,975
When the obstructive uropathy is relieved, diuresis ensues and hypertension dissipates.

614
01:18:56,975 --> 01:19:14,300
In one woman with massive hydramnios (9.4 L) and an anencephalic fetus, amniocentesis and removal of some of the amnionic fluid was followed promptly by diuresis, a decline in the plasma creatinine concentration, and an improvement of hypertension.

615
01:19:14,300 --> 01:19:17,112
Lower Urinary Tract Lesions.

616
01:19:17,112 --> 01:19:20,000
# Urethral Diverticulum.

617
01:19:20,000 --> 01:19:30,512
Infrequently complicating pregnancy, this type of diverticulum is thought to originate from an enlarging paraurethral gland abscess that ruptures into the urethral lumen .

618
01:19:30,512 --> 01:19:37,662
As infection clears, the remaining dilated diverticular sac and its ostium into the urethra persist.

619
01:19:37,662 --> 01:19:46,962
Associated findings may be urine collecting within and then dribbling from the sac, pain, palpable mass, and recurrent urinary infections.

620
01:19:46,962 --> 01:19:51,962
In general, a diverticulum is managed expectantly during pregnancy.

621
01:19:51,962 --> 01:19:56,487
Rarely, drainage may be necessary, or surgery required .

622
01:19:56,487 --> 01:20:10,975
If additional antepartum evaluation is needed, MR imaging is preferred for its superior soft-tissue resolution and ability to define complex diverticula (Dwarkasing, 2011; Pathi, 2013).

623
01:20:10,975 --> 01:20:13,687
Figure 56-6.

624
01:20:13,687 --> 01:20:23,737
Asymptomatic urethral diverticulum, seen as a midline bulge beneath the urethral meatus (arrow), identified during initial prenatal care examination.

625
01:20:23,737 --> 01:20:26,875
# Urinary Tract Fistulas.

626
01:20:26,875 --> 01:20:34,137
Fistulas found during pregnancy likely existed previously, but in rare cases, they form during pregnancy.

627
01:20:34,137 --> 01:20:40,550
In developed countries, vesicovaginal fistula following a McDonald cerclage has been reported .

628
01:20:40,550 --> 01:20:47,600
These fistulas may also form with the prolonged obstructed labor that is more commonly seen in resource-poor countries.

629
01:20:47,600 --> 01:20:53,587
In these cases, the genital tract is compressed between the fetal head and bony pelvis.

630
01:20:53,587 --> 01:21:01,087
Brief pressure is not significant, but prolonged pressure leads to tissue necrosis and subsequent fistula formation .

631
01:21:01,087 --> 01:21:11,312
Vesicouterine fistulas that developed after prior cesarean delivery have been described (DiMarco, 2006; Manjunatha, 2012).

632
01:21:11,312 --> 01:21:20,800
Rarely, vesicocervical fistula may follow cesarean delivery or may form if the anterior cervical lip is compressed against the symphysis pubis .

